@@ -0,0 +1,81 @@
+# Rejected: arXiv 2011.08447
+
+Paper: Yash Khanna, [*Exact recovery of planted cliques in semi-random graphs*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.08447), arXiv:2011.08447v5.
+
+## Decision
+
+No family is shipped. Generatability and verification are straightforward: sample a
+set `S`, add every edge inside `S`, and use the vertex list as a certificate; a checker
+only needs to check its size, distinctness, range, and all induced edges. The failure is
+**H**, under both possible track claims.
+
+- **Track A fails in the paper's recovery regime.** Definition 2 gives the exact
+  semi-random distribution. Theorem 2 explicitly gives a deterministic polynomial-time
+  algorithm that recovers `S` with high probability when
+  `p >= kappa log(n)/n` and `nu in (0,1)`, including the stated regime
+  `k = Omega(max(sqrt(np(r+t+2)), lambda))` with the accompanying sparsity
+  conditions. Section 2 and Algorithm 1 say what produces the certificate: solve SDP 1,
+  threshold the vector norms, and greedily complete the clique. Thus the distribution
+  for which the paper proves recovery is not a Track A hard distribution.
+- **Track B fails because there is no compact route.** The paper's insight explains why
+  the SDP mass concentrates on the plant, but it does not replace the SDP computation.
+  Algorithm 1's threshold and greedy completion start only after the SDP solution has
+  been obtained. No invariant, symmetry, change of variables, or other by-hand shortcut
+  in the paper identifies the clique from the input graph in at most 300 exact
+  operations.
+
+Using `r=t=0` below the recovery threshold does not repair the claim. Section 1.4 then
+reduces the model to ordinary planted clique and discusses the familiar
+`k=o(sqrt(n))` barrier (including a cited sum-of-squares lower bound), but the paper
+does not prove distributional hardness there. More importantly for this benchmark,
+moving below the threshold removes the recovery theorem without supplying a compact
+answer-producing route: the solver is left with clique search. That is not Track B
+compression, and worst-case NP-hardness of clique does not establish Track A hardness
+for the generated distribution.
+
+## Mechanical cost versus compact route
+
+To make the comparison concrete, consider a modest would-be shipping instance with
+`n=64`, `k=16`, `p=1/8`, and `r=t=0`. Its structure-aware answer space already has
+`C(64,16) = 488,526,937,079,580` vertex sets, so it is large enough for the guess gate.
+
+The standard method in Section 2 is not a small hand computation. Writing SDP 1 as a
+Gram-matrix program uses a PSD matrix of order 65, hence 2,145 independent scalar
+entries. Even before the nonedge equations, its displayed constraints contribute at
+least 8,258 scalar equalities/inequalities: 8,064 ordered-pair bound inequalities and
+194 normalization, row-sum, diagonal, and anchor constraints. At `p=1/8`, this example
+has about 1,659 additional nonedge orthogonality equations in expectation. A single
+dense cubic PSD factorization at order 65 is about 91,541 scalar arithmetic operations;
+an SDP algorithm performs repeated separation/linear-algebra steps. The paper states
+polynomial-time solvability by the ellipsoid algorithm but gives no smaller exact
+operation count.
+
+The alleged compact route is **no shorter**: none is supplied independently of that
+SDP. After the SDP, Algorithm 1 still performs 64 threshold comparisons and up to
+`n*k = 1,024` adjacency tests for greedy completion, already beyond the 300-operation
+cap; obtaining the threshold set first still requires the full program above. Brute
+force instead examines a language of 488,526,937,079,580 candidates. Therefore the
+mechanical route and the shortest answer-producing route are the same SDP/search-scale
+calculation, not a large mechanical computation hiding a short structural solution.
+
+This is why the efficient algorithm is not being used as a one-line reason to reject:
+its mechanical cost is indeed far beyond unaided hand execution, but the second number
+required for Track B--the compact-route length--does not shrink. It remains above
+roughly 90,000 arithmetic operations for even one dense PSD step (plus at least 9,917
+displayed scalar constraints in the example), rather than at most 300.
+
+## Paper locations relied on
+
+- Definition 2 (Section 1.2): the seven-stage semi-random graph model, including the
+  monotone edge-deletion adversary.
+- Theorem 2 (Section 1.3): deterministic polynomial-time exact recovery in the stated
+  parameter regime.
+- Section 1.4: ordinary planted-clique special case, known easy thresholds, and the
+  cited below-threshold sum-of-squares limitation.
+- Section 1.5: the SDP-mass/long-vector proof idea.
+- Section 1.6, Lemma 1: monotone-adversary robustness.
+- Section 2, Lemma 3: the long-vector threshold set is a subset of the plant.
+- Section 2, Algorithm 1: SDP solve, norm thresholding, and greedy completion.
+
+No generator module was written, so there is no partially built module to retain and no
+LLM hardening transcript to report.
